window.docContents[36].resolve({"/Deploying-a-domain-model___-the-deployment-template/The-platform/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Deploying-a-domain-model___-the-deployment-template--The-platform":{"contents":"* D9 — The image facts. What ships per unit, the toolchain and architecture spectrum it\n  was built for, and the ABI floor it runs against. The reason to state the spectrum\n  rather than \"it builds\" is that the development box and the deployment image routinely\n  differ in both compiler version and target set, and either difference can hide a failure\n  that cannot appear locally.* D10 — Mechanized registration and version derivation. Registration and version\n  derivation are mechanized, so a deployed version is derived from the artifact rather\n  than asserted beside it.* D11 — Backend interchange. What backend interchange means for this deployment: what\n  the same source does on each target, and what changes when the target does.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Deploying a domain model: the deployment template","header":"33.5. The platform","id":"/Deploying-a-domain-model___-the-deployment-template/The-platform/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Deploying-a-domain-model___-the-deployment-template--The-platform"},"/Requirements/Verified-classification___-kind-laws-that-instantiate-at-the-quantity-level-_LPAR_R12_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Requirements--Verified-classification___-kind-laws-that-instantiate-at-the-quantity-level-_LPAR_R12_RPAR_":{"contents":"R12 — A classification is a certificate, not an assertion; kind-laws instantiate at\nthe quantity level. By default a kind is a tag: Quantity k R records the kind but\nthe magnitude is arbitrary, so a value can be labelled with a kind it has not earned.\nR12 upgrades a classification to a certificate — a proof that a quantity satisfies\nits kind's defining relation (the formalized ISO 80000 Remark) to quantities of the\nkinds it is built from. A length built as speed × time is certified by construction; a\nbare ⟨999⟩ cannot be certified against those factors. The kind becomes a refinement\n(a tag plus its earned proof).\n\nThe reason this strengthens the calculus is instantiation. Every kind-law is stated\nas a carrier-parametric universal over quantities with the kind-relation as a premise,\nso it instantiates to a quantity-level fact by ordinary application — the inter-kind\ngeneralization of R10's carrier-parametric laws — and any property of a kind's defining\nrelation transports to every quantity certified under it (every certified surface\narea is ≥ 0, because the area element is). The pattern comes in three algebraic\nshapes — product (area = length · length, energy = force · length), quotient\n(speed = length / duration, plane angle = arc / radius), and reciprocal (frequency =\n1 / period, curvature = 1 / radius) — each a kind-law family in the core; analysis-shaped\nrelations such as area as a surface integral ∬ √g du dv follow the same discipline in\nthe layer where their mathematics lives. These are exercised directly on ISO 80000-3's\nown Remarks (see the ISO 80000-3 chapter), where, for instance, the plane angle is\ncomputed to be dimension one because it is a ratio of two lengths. R12 unifies the two\nhalves of the standards work: the formalized remark is the defining relation, and\nclassification is the witnessed instantiation of it.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Requirements","header":"2.8. Verified classification: kind-laws that instantiate at the quantity level (R12)","id":"/Requirements/Verified-classification___-kind-laws-that-instantiate-at-the-quantity-level-_LPAR_R12_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Requirements--Verified-classification___-kind-laws-that-instantiate-at-the-quantity-level-_LPAR_R12_RPAR_"},"/The-Function-Calculus-and-Complex-Valued-Carriers-_LPAR_R12_RPAR_/Functions-on-quantities/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--The-Function-Calculus-and-Complex-Valued-Carriers-_LPAR_R12_RPAR_--Functions-on-quantities":{"contents":"\n\n is the function operation surface a kind-indexed magnitude\nneeds — , , , , the hyperbolic functions, ,\n, and the constant  — supplied once per representation type . It is the\nfunction-layer analogue of the numeric carrier Carrier, and is by construction the\nsame surface as the underlying tensor toolchain's elementary functions, so every carrier\nthat supports them — , Float, the binary32 carriers — is a clean\n.\n\nRealized as the MathCarrier class. The richer surface (, the inverse trig,\n, cube root, rounding, rational power) is split off into\nMathCarrierExt, so the executable binary32 carrier — whose function vocabulary is\ndeliberately smaller — is a MathCarrier without being a MathCarrierExt. The algebraic\nidentities (, ) live in\nLawfulMathCarrier, which holds at  and fails in floating point — the same\nexec/spec gap the lawful numeric carrier draws for . The Float\ninstance is computable (the run carrier); the  instance is the noncomputable\nproof carrier in the PhysLib-backed Dimension layer.\n\n records that raising a -quantity to the rational\npower  yields a -quantity (). In the core it carries only the\nratio-scale gate (Dybkær §13.3.5 — only ratio quantities have powers); the\ndimension layer strengthens it to the exponent-scaling certificate\n. This is the one function family that is not\ndimension-one-in-out — the dimension-scaling extension of multiplication and division.\n\nRealized as the PowerKind structure (two ratio-scale fields), with the smart constructors\nQuantity.sqrt (the half-power), Quantity.cbrt, and\nQuantity.rpow. The exponent-scaling refinement is DimPowerKind, well-defined because\nPhysLib's Dimension carries  exponents (Pow Dimension ℚ), making a\nhalf-power a genuine group element.\n\n is the verified half-power, the canonical\nPowerKind  smart constructor:  of a \nyields a , its magnitude  taken in the carrier. It rides on the base\nMathCarrier (not the extended surface), so it runs even on the\nbinary32 executable carrier.\n\nRealized as Quantity.sqrt, taking a PowerKind (1/2) k₁ k and returning\n; the projection\nsqrt_magnitude is rfl, so an erased computation is exactly the carrier's\n.\n\nThe square root of an area is a length — checked, not annotated. The half-power of the\narea dimension  is the length dimension :\n\nThe certificate Quantity.sqrt carries, here discharged in the\nPhysLib Dimension group. Builds on the power law.\n\nProved in the Function module: areaK is declared with dimension  and the\ngoal lengthKind.dim = areaK.dim ^ (1/2 : ℚ) is closed component-wise by norm_num over\nthe rational exponents of the Dimension group.\n\n licenses the smart constructors\nfor , , the hyperbolic and trigonometric\nfunctions. The core records the ratio-scale gate; the dimension layer\nstrengthens it to \"argument and result are dimension one\" (Families C), and — for the\ntrigonometric functions — to a curated algebra (the unary analogue of\nthe interaction algebra) that keeps  on plane angles only. That a\nplane angle is dimension one yet a distinct kind is exactly why  of an angle is\nfine while  of a reflectivity is the category error a dimension-only system cannot\nforbid.\n\nRealized as TranscendentalKind with the constructors Quantity.exp / log / sin /\ncos / tanh (base surface) and tan / asin / acos / atan / atan2 (extended\nsurface). The dimensional strengthening is DimTranscendental (tanh_lossTangent_coherent\nis the worked edge: the conductive-loss  argument is dimensionless and so is its\nvalue), and the trigonometric curation is the Dimension-layer trig algebra.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009The Function Calculus and Complex-Valued Carriers (R12)","header":"12.1. Functions on quantities","id":"/The-Function-Calculus-and-Complex-Valued-Carriers-_LPAR_R12_RPAR_/Functions-on-quantities/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--The-Function-Calculus-and-Complex-Valued-Carriers-_LPAR_R12_RPAR_--Functions-on-quantities"}});